<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amadeus VII, Count of Savoy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1360">1360</a>–<a href="/years/1391">1391</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as the Red Count · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amadeus VII, called the Red Count, was count of Savoy from 1383 to 1391. In 1388 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> and its hinterland placed themselves under his protection in the act known as the Dedition, giving Savoy an outlet to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> that it kept until 1860. He died after a hunting accident in 1391.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amadeus was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a> on 24 February 1360, the son of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-vi-count-of-savoy">Amadeus VI of Savoy</a> and Bonne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>. In 1382, summoned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france">Charles VI of France</a>, he led Savoyard troops to victory against Flemish rebels at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-roosebeke">Battle of Roosebeke</a>. After his father's death in 1383 his mother and grandmother fought over the regency.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1384 Amadeus led an army to suppress a revolt against his relative Edward of Savoy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Sion, and the army attacked and pillaged Sion. In 1388 he acquired territories in eastern Provence and the port city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, which gave the County of Savoy access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and control of an important Alpine pass. Nice became part of the domains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a> on 28 September 1388. Amadeus took advantage of internal struggles in Provence and negotiated the transfer of Nice and the Ubaye Valley with Giovanni Grimaldi, baron of Boglio and governor of Nice and Eastern Provence. The new lands were called the Terre Nuove di Provenza.</p><p>The pass, the Col de Largentière, now the Maddalena Pass on the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, was of great strategic and commercial importance because it linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> with Italy. Amadeus was known for his hospitality, and never turned a person from his table without a meal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 1 November 1391 Amadeus died of tetanus after a hunting accident. His will caused controversy. He named his own mother, a sister of the Duke de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, as guardian of his son and heir Amadeus VIII, instead of following the tradition of appointing the child's mother, Bonne of Berry, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-duke-of-berry">John, Duke of Berry</a>. Because of the dispute between the two women, rumours that Amadeus had been poisoned soon emerged, and three months of negotiation were needed to restore peace in the family. His son Amadeus VIII was later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-viii-duke-of-savoy">Antipope Felix V</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amadeus VII of Savoy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the war of succession that followed Queen Joanna I's death, Nice and its hinterland rejected the Angevin claimant and in 1388, in the act known as the Dedition, placed themselves under <b>Amadeus VII of Savoy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-nice">County of Nice</a></li><li><blockquote>Amadeus VI, the Green Count, led a crusade in 1366, and <b>Amadeus VII</b> acquired Nice in 1388.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-savoy">County of Savoy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in castle of Ripaille, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.57,5.92&amp;year=1380" aria-label="Chambéry on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Amadeus VII, Count of Savoy"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5354 6206h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5354 6206h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.57,5.92&amp;year=1380">What was here in 1380? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
